        "reasoning_content": "The user wants me to route a batch of 60 sources (P0301-P0360) to the appropriate chapters in a shared outline for a review on \"How does the gut microbiome modulate the efficacy of immune checkpoint inhibitors in solid tumours, and what mechanisms mediate that effect?\"\n\nThe shared outline has 5 chapters:\n- Ch2_Clinical: Clinical association evidence between gut microbiome and ICI efficacy\n- Ch3_Mechanisms: Molecular and cellular mechanisms of microbiome modulation\n- Ch4_Context: Contextual factors affecting microbiome effects (tumor type, treatment, antibiotics, host factors)\n- Ch5_Interventions: Clinical translation of microbiome interventions (FMT, probiotics, diet)\n- Ch6_Challenges: Methodological challenges and future directions\n\nI need to read each source's B_review_planning and assign it to appropriate chapter(s) based on its actual content, research type, object, conditions, and depth. I should not just rely on broad claims but check what the paper actually provides.\n\nLet me go through each source:\n\nP0301: HCC study, exploratory, found no association between microbiome diversity and ICI efficacy in HCC. Good for Ch4 (context - tumor type specificity, HCC as negative/limiting case) and Ch6 (methodological limitations).\n\nP0302: Salivary microbiome in oral cancer with chemoradiotherapy, not ICI. Limited relevance. Could go to Ch6 as a cautionary example about site-specificity, or Ch4 for context about non-gut microbiomes. But since it's not ICI and not gut, relevance is limited.\n\nP0303: 2023 review on gut microbiome in cancer immunotherapy. Good foundational source for Ch2 (clinical associations), Ch3 (mechanisms), Ch5 (interventions), Ch6 (challenges).\n\nP0304: 2022 systematic review on antibiotics and FMT impact on ICI. Good for Ch2 (clinical evidence), Ch4 (antibiotics as context), Ch5 (FMT interventions), Ch6 (limitations).\n\n\nP0305: 2025 study on engineered bacteria producing IAA metabolite for tumor immun